course: dictionary
lesson: 14028
title: Malleable: easy to shape
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Malleable» describes something that can be hammered, pressed or bent into a new shape without breaking. Gold and copper are malleable metals: you can beat them into thin sheets. Clay is malleable too, because you can squash it and mould it into a bowl.
explain: Look at the clues around the word: «The goldsmith beat the gold into a thin, flat sheet.» Beating is hitting, and the gold did not crack or fall apart, so it must be malleable. Glass would shatter, so glass is not malleable.
explain: The word also describes people. «Young minds are malleable» means young people can easily be changed or influenced by others, a bit like clay. Check it by swapping in a word such as easily influenced: if the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: «Malleable» means able to be hammered, pressed or bent into a new shape without breaking.
fact: In science, «malleable» is a key property of metals such as gold, copper and aluminium.
fact: When used about people, «malleable» means easily changed or influenced by other people.
fact: «Brittle» is the opposite: a brittle material breaks easily, like glass or a dry biscuit.
fact: «Ductile» means able to be pulled into a wire, which is a different property from malleable.
exercise: «The goldsmith beat the gold into a thin, flat sheet.» Why is gold malleable? | It melts in the sun | It is very light | It is always shiny | *It can be hammered into a new shape without breaking
exercise: «Glass is brittle, so it breaks easily.» Which science word means the opposite of malleable? | Ductile | *Brittle | Shiny | Heavy
exercise: Lily says, «The toddler's mind is malleable.» What does Lily mean? | Her mind is made of metal | Her mind is very tired | *Her mind is easy to influence and change | Her mind cannot learn anything
test: «Clay is malleable, so you can squash it and mould it into a bowl.» What can clay do because it is malleable? | *Be shaped without breaking | Shine like gold | Float on water | Carry electricity through a wire
test: Mum says, «My brother is malleable, so he will try any idea.» What does malleable mean here? | *Easy to persuade or influence | Very tall for his age | Sleepy and slow | Hard, like steel
exam: Which is the best reason a scientist would call copper malleable? | *It can be hammered into thin sheets without breaking | It is a liquid at room temperature | It floats on water | It is see-through, like glass
